  "text": "The **water cycle**, also known as the **hydrologic cycle**, is the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. It is a closed system, meaning the total amount of water on Earth remains relatively constant, but its state (solid, liquid, gas) and location change constantly.\n\nThe cycle is driven primarily by **solar energy** (which powers evaporation) and **gravity** (which pulls water downward).\n\nBelow is a detailed explanation of the key processes, followed by real-world examples.\n\n---\n\n### Key Processes of the Water Cycle\n\n#### 1. Evaporation\n- **Definition**: The process by which water changes from a liquid to a gas (water vapor).\n- **Mechanism**: Solar energy heats water in oceans, lakes, and rivers, causing molecules to gain enough energy to escape into the atmosphere.\n- **Example**: On a hot summer day, puddles on the street disappear as the sun heats them, turning the water into invisible vapor.\n\n#### 2. Transpiration\n- **Definition**: The release of water vapor from plants and soil into the atmosphere.\n- **Mechanism**: Plants absorb water through their roots and release it through tiny pores (stomata)",
