  "text": "A Transformer is a deep learning architecture introduced in the 2017 paper *\"Attention Is All You Need\"* by Vaswani et al. It revolutionized Natural Language Processing (NLP) and is the backbone of modern Large Language Models (LLMs) like GPT, BERT, and Llama.\n\nUnlike previous architectures (RNNs/LSTMs), Transformers process all input tokens **in parallel**, not sequentially, making them highly efficient for training on large datasets.\n\nHere is a step-by-step explanation of how a Transformer works, focusing on the **Encoder-Decoder** structure (though many modern models use only one part).\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Input Embedding\nThe model cannot process raw text directly. It must convert words into numerical vectors.\n\n1.  **Tokenization**: The input sentence is split into tokens (words, sub-words, or characters).\n   - Example: `\"Hello world\"` \u2192 `[\"Hello\", \"world\"]`\n2.  **Embedding**: Each token is mapped to a high-dimensional vector (e.g., 512 or 768 dimensions) using a learned embedding matrix.\n   - Result: A matrix of shape `(sequence_length, embedding_dim)",
